---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
// Customer privacy notice, tightened from the ICO's generator output on
// 2026-07-21. Substance (purposes, lawful bases, rights, ICO details) must
// track what the generator produced — tighten wording, don't change meaning.
// The "Cookies and embedded content" section is ours, not the generator's:
// it exists because /book/ embeds Google's scheduler (added 2026-08-21).
// If the booking provider changes, that section changes with it.
// Deliberately company-voiced ("we" = Async Forest Ltd), overriding the
// STYLESEED "you"/"I" rule: legal pages must not frame the company as an
// individual (corporate-separation requirement — see STYLESEED voice note).
---

<Base
  title="Privacy notice — Async Forest"
  description="How Async Forest Ltd collects, uses, keeps, and shares personal information, and the data protection rights you have over it."
>
  <section class="privacy container container--narrow">
    <div class="privacy__head">
      <h1>Privacy notice</h1>
      <p class="privacy__lede">
        This notice tells you what to expect Async Forest Ltd ("we") to do
        with your personal information, and the rights you have over it.
        Async Forest Ltd is registered in England and Wales, company
        no. 17351665, and is the data controller for the personal
        information described here.
      </p>
      <p class="privacy__updated">Last updated 21 August 2026</p>
    </div>

    <div class="privacy__body">
      <h2>Contact details</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:henry@asyncforest.com">henry@asyncforest.com</a>
        for anything in this notice, including rights requests and complaints.
      </p>

      <h2>What we collect, and why</h2>
      <p>To provide and improve consulting services for clients, we collect and use:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>names and contact details</li>
        <li>addresses</li>
        <li>occupation</li>
        <li>payment details, including card or bank information for transfers and direct debits</li>
        <li>transaction data — payments to and from you, and the services you have purchased</li>
        <li>records of meetings and decisions</li>
        <li>website usage information — how you interact with and use the site and our services</li>
        <li>information relating to compliments or complaints</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For information updates and marketing, we collect and use:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>names and contact details</li>
        <li>addresses</li>
        <li>marketing preferences</li>
        <li>website user journey information</li>
        <li>IP addresses</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For dealing with queries, complaints, or claims, we collect and use
        names and contact details.
      </p>

      <h2>Where we get personal information from</h2>
      <p>Directly from you. We don't buy in or scrape personal information.</p>

      <h2>Lawful bases</h2>
      <p>
        Under UK data protection law we must have a lawful basis for
        collecting and using your personal information. We rely on three,
        and which one applies affects which of your rights apply:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Consent</strong> — you have given us permission after we
          gave you all the relevant information. You can withdraw consent at
          any time. All of your data protection rights may apply, except the
          right to object.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contract</strong> — we need the information to enter into
          or carry out a contract with you. All of your rights may apply,
          except the right to object.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Legal obligation</strong> — we have to collect or use the
          information to comply with the law. All of your rights may apply,
          except the rights to erasure, to object, and to data portability.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>In particular:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>providing and improving services for clients — consent, contract, and legal obligation</li>
        <li>information updates and marketing — consent</li>
        <li>queries, complaints, or claims — consent and legal obligation</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Your data protection rights</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Access</strong> — ask for copies of your personal information, and for details such as where we got it and who we share it with.</li>
        <li><strong>Rectification</strong> — ask us to correct information you think is inaccurate, or complete information you think is incomplete.</li>
        <li><strong>Erasure</strong> — ask us to delete your personal information.</li>
        <li><strong>Restriction</strong> — ask us to limit how we can use your personal information.</li>
        <li><strong>Objection</strong> — object to our processing of your personal information.</li>
        <li><strong>Portability</strong> — ask us to transfer the personal information you gave us to another organisation, or to you.</li>
        <li><strong>Withdrawing consent</strong> — where we rely on consent, withdraw it at any time.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Exemptions apply to some of these rights, so you may not always
        receive everything you ask for. The
        <a href="https://ico.org.uk/for-the-public/" rel="noopener">ICO's guidance</a>
        explains the rights and exemptions in full. If you make a request,
        we must respond without undue delay, and in any event within one
        month — contact us at the email address above.
      </p>

      <h2>How long we keep information</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Client and engagement records</strong> — contracts, correspondence, meeting notes, and deliverables: six years after the engagement ends.</li>
        <li><strong>Financial and transaction records</strong> — invoices and payment details: six years after the end of the financial year they relate to, as UK tax and company law requires.</li>
        <li><strong>Enquiries that don't become engagements</strong> — twelve months after the last contact.</li>
        <li><strong>Marketing information</strong> — until you unsubscribe or withdraw consent.</li>
        <li><strong>Complaint records</strong> — six years after the complaint is resolved.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For more on how long we keep specific information, or the criteria
        we use to decide, contact us at the email address above.
      </p>

      <h2>Who we share information with</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Our accountants</strong>, who process accounting records on our behalf as a data processor.</li>
        <li><strong>Professional or legal advisers</strong>, where an engagement or dispute requires it.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google</strong>, which provides the appointment scheduling
          embedded on our booking page — see the section below.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Cookies and embedded content</h2>
      <p>
        We set no cookies of our own, and we use no advertising or cross-site
        tracking. Any analytics we run are cookie-less and do not identify
        individual visitors.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our <a href="/book/">booking page</a> is the one exception: it embeds
        Google Calendar's appointment scheduling. Opening that page contacts
        Google's servers, which receive your IP address and browser details
        and may set their own cookies in your browser. If you book a time, the
        name, email address, and any notes you enter go to Google Calendar and
        to us, and we use them to arrange and prepare for the meeting. Google
        acts as an independent controller for what it collects through the
        embed, on its own terms — see the
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google privacy policy</a>.
        If you would rather not load the embed, email us instead at the
        address above and we will arrange a time directly. No other page on
        this site loads content from Google.
      </p>

      <h2>How to complain</h2>
      <p>
        If you have any concerns about our use of your personal information,
        complain to us first at
        <a href="mailto:henry@asyncforest.com">henry@asyncforest.com</a>.
        If you remain unhappy after that, you can complain to the
        Information Commissioner's Office:
      </p>
      <p>
        Information Commissioner's Office<br />
        Wycliffe House, Water Lane<br />
        Wilmslow, Cheshire, SK9 5AF<br />
        Helpline: 0303 123 1113<br />
        <a href="https://www.ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint" rel="noopener">ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .privacy {
    padding-block: var(--section-y) var(--space-8);
  }
  .privacy__head {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }
  .privacy__lede {
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 58ch;
  }
  .privacy__updated {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .privacy__body h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-10);
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
  }
  .privacy__body ul {
    max-width: var(--container-prose);
  }
</style>
